WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.800
Film Courage: What did writing your first
screenplay teach you about screenwriting?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.330
Mark Harris, Filmmaker: Oh, gosh!

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:09.151
So when I wrote my first screenplay…now
this is the different between writing a first

00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:12.110
screenplay and when I was directing.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:20.330
When I wrote my first screenplay of course
Syd Field is like…I don’t think you can

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.730
really be a solid screenwriter if you haven’t
studied Syd Field.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:29.249
I’ve read a lot of screenplays, tons of
screenplays before I even started writing

00:00:29.249 --> 00:00:30.249
screenplays.

00:00:30.249 --> 00:00:34.990
I’ve read Syd Field’s book and I continue
to read his books.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.390
But tons of screenplays.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:43.290
When I wrote my first screenplay (and it never
got produced of course) and I took it out

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.920
and people read it, it was pretty decent but
it wasn’t good.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:59.150
It was only after writing at least 10 screenplays
before I actually produced my own stuff.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.700
But it taught me a lot.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:07.549
That I didn’t play with at all because I
knew that in order to sit down and write there

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:09.689
had to be a particular structure.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:14.780
And that’s one of the things too is that,
one of these things is very important as screenwriters

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:22.109
and filmmakers is that when you’re pitching
to producers you have to make sure that the

00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:33.109
screenplay is properly structured, use Final
Draft and we have to really, really invest

00:01:33.109 --> 00:01:34.619
in ourselves and in our craft.

00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:38.950
Go out and get the Final Draft program, go
out and read Syd Field’s book and know how

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.369
to read screenplays.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:47.579
And make sure that when you actually pitch
to producers that it’s properly formatted.

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:53.710
There is nothing worse than someone saying
they have an idea and they pitch you an idea

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.270
but they want you to write the idea.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:04.619
Like I have tons of screenplays of my own
or screenwriters who want you to read their

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:10.150
screenplays but it’s not properly formatted,
I’ve had that.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:15.640
Recently someone asked me to read their screenplay,
they had the heading and it was written like

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:16.640
a play.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.709
I’m like there’s a difference between
a screenplay and a stage play.

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:27.860
And I don’t think people understand that
when you’re writing a screenplay, it’s

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:34.770
formatted a particular way because when you
are dealing with different set ups those are

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:35.770
very important.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.780
And I had to explain it to the person.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.250
He’s like “Just read it.”

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:45.069
I was like “For me as a filmmaker, that’s
hard to follow.”

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:49.750
When you don’t have the proper headings
on it like INTERIOR: I don’t know where

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.459
I’m at in the story.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:53.590
I can’t follow it.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:03:01.849
But those are very important because now this
is one set-up, we go outside that’s a different

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:02.849
set-up.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:10.269
So you have to make sure that it’s in the
screenplay as an exterior so I’ve read screenplays

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:11.390
where they don’t have that.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.040
It’s like there’s no way.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.670
And people don’t understand because they
think you’re hating because they’re making

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:24.469
it difficult to read their screenplay because
they don’t want to follow the proper structure.

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:29.209
You follow the proper structure and it will
make it much easier for us to read your screenplay.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:33.870
Film Courage: How was it for you to get your
first feedback because you spoke of feedback

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:38.109
earlier in the interview and how it’s important.

00:03:38.109 --> 00:03:44.790
Was the first time you showed someone your
screenplay, what was that feedback?

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:53.409
Mark: The feedback I got was…again it’s
something that you have to grow and evolve

00:03:53.409 --> 00:04:02.060
but when somebody tells you this is not properly
done or the story is not connecting, you are

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.060
like “Why?

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.060
Why?”

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.060
You get offended by it.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.910
But the thing is we can’t be because if
we want to grow and evolve, we have to listen

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.650
to constructive criticism.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:17.560
And if someone is giving you some constructive
criticism, I know it’s hard for us to deal

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.510
with it, it was hard for me to deal with it.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:30.090
It’s like still today I have to be more
open-minded when people read my screenplays.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:43.800
You have to train your mind or keep telling
myself that this is necessary, this feedback

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.350
is necessary.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.509
I was just watching the movie THE WIFE.

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:52.600
Film Courage: Oh yes!

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.600
Excellent.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.600
Mark: Excellent movie.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.680
And when his wife gave him that feedback he
just blew up, right?

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.640
He didn’t want other that feedback.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.280
But at the end of the day if he had listened
to her, he probably would have become a better

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.610
writer instead of what happened.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:24.970
But we have to listen to those voices that
give us that constructive criticism so we

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:25.970
can evolve.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:27.960
I know it’s difficult.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:32.090
Even today, when I’m letting someone read
my screenplay and they’re giving me feedback

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:39.690
on it, I have to keep telling myself over
and over again “This is necessary.”

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:45.630
When that feedback is difficult to hear, just
repeat “I need this.”

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.759
Because it’s very important.

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:54.460
It’s to allow us to grow and evolve and
just put the ego aside.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:59.620
We have to put that ego aside at all times
even while on set, put the ego aside and listen

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.340
to the people who can make our projects better.

